 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know Joshua and Caleb took the spies' portions?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Ulla derives it from חיו.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_batra.118b.3",
   "daf": "118b",
   "sentenceId": 2,
   "he": "מְרַגְּלִים – יְהוֹשֻׁעַ וְכָלֵב נָטְלוּ חֶלְקָם. מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר עוּלָּא, דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן נוּן וְכָלֵב בֶּן יְפֻנֶּה חָיוּ מִן הָאֲנָשִׁים הָהֵם״ – מַאי ״חָיוּ״? אִילֵּימָא חָיוּ מַמָּשׁ, וְהָא כְּתִיב קְרָא אַחֲרִינָא: ״וְלֹא נוֹתַר מֵהֶם אִישׁ, כִּי אִם כָּלֵב בֶּן יְפֻנֶּה וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן נוּן״! אֶלָּא מַאי ״חָיוּ״ – שֶׁחָיוּ בְּחֶלְקָם.",
   "en": "§ The Gemara analyzes the next section of the baraita, which states: With regard to the twelve «spies» sent to survey Eretz Yisrael prior to the Jewish people’s entry into the land, «Joshua and Caleb took» all of the spies’ «portions» of the land. The Gemara asks: «From where is this matter» derived? «Ulla said:» It is «as the verse states: “But Joshua, son of Nun, and Caleb, son of Jephunneh, lived of those men» that went to spy out the land” (Numbers 14:38). «What» does the term «“lived”» mean? «If we say» that it means «literally» that «they lived, but» there is «another verse» that «states: “And there was not left a man of them, save Caleb, son of Jephunneh, and Joshua, son of Nun”» (Numbers 26:65), so why would the Torah state it twice? «Rather, what» does the term «“lived”» mean? «That» Joshua and Caleb «lived in» the other spies’ «portion» of the land."
  }
 ],
